Enforcement Decree of the Framework Act on Agriculture, Rural Community and Food Industry

Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters delegated by the Framework Act on Agriculture, Rural Community and Food Industry and matters necessary for its enforcement. <Amended on December 22, 2015>

Article 2 (Scope of Agriculture)
Agriculture under subparagraph 1 of Article 3 of the Framework Act on Agriculture, Rural Community and Food Industry (hereinafter referred to as the "Act") shall consist of the following: <Amended on December 22, 2015; October 8, 2019>
1. Crop cultivation: Cultivation of food crops, vegetables, fruit, flowers, special-purpose crops, medicinal crops, fodder crops, green manure crops, and mushrooms; sericulture; and cultivation of seeds and seedlings (excluding the cultivation of seeds and seedlings for forestry);
2. Livestock farming: Raising, propagation, hatching, and breeding-stock farming of animals (excluding aquatic animals);
3. Forestry: Silviculture (including cultivation of seeds and seedlings for forestry and the creation, management, or operation of natural recreation forests, arboretums, and gardens under the Forestry Culture and Recreation Act and the Act on the Creation and Furtherance of Arboretums and Gardens), and production and gathering of forest products.

Article 3 (Criteria for Farmers)
(1) "Persons who meet the criteria prescribed by Presidential Decree" in subparagraph 2 of Article 3 of the Act means persons who fall under any of the following subparagraphs: <Amended on December 15, 2009; December 22, 2015>
1. A person who operates or cultivates at least 1,000 square meters of farmland (excluding farmland attached to rural houses, etc. allotted or leased to non-farmers under Article 98 of the Agricultural and Fishing Villages Improvement Act);
2. A person whose annual sales of agricultural products from agricultural management amount to at least 1.2 million won;
3. A person engaged in agriculture for at least 90 days a year;
4. A person continuously employed for at least one year in the shipment, distribution, processing, or export of agricultural products by an agricultural cooperative corporation established under Article 16 (1) of the Act on Fostering and Supporting Agricultural and Fisheries Business Entities;
5. A person continuously employed for at least one year in the distribution, processing, or sale of agricultural products by an agricultural company corporation under Article 19 (1) of the Act on Fostering and Supporting Agricultural and Fisheries Business Entities.
(2) Deleted. <December 22, 2015>
(3) Matters necessary concerning methods of confirming farmers under paragraph (1), etc. shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on March 23, 2013; December 22, 2015>

Article 4 (Scope of Producers' Organizations)
"Organizations prescribed by Presidential Decree" in subparagraph 4 of Article 3 of the Act means the following organizations: <Amended on March 23, 2013; December 22, 2015>
1. Cooperatives and their National Federation under the Agricultural Cooperatives Act;
2. Forestry cooperatives and their National Federation under the Forestry Cooperatives Act;
3. Leaf tobacco production cooperatives and their National Federation under the Leaf Tobacco Production Cooperatives Act;
4. Deleted. <December 22, 2015>
5. Specialized producers' organizations with legal personality, formed by at least five farmers to produce agricultural products jointly or to produce agricultural products and sell, process, or export them jointly, which meet the requirements determined by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs.

Article 5 (Scope of Agricultural and Fishery Products)
(1) "Products prescribed by Presidential Decree" in subparagraph 6 (a) of Article 3 of the Act means products produced through agricultural activities under Article 2.
(2) "Products prescribed by Presidential Decree" in subparagraph 6 (b) of Article 3 of the Act means products produced through fishery activities under subparagraph 1 (a) of Article 3 of the Framework Act on Fisheries and Fishing Villages Development. <Amended on December 22, 2015>

Article 6 (Scope of the Food Industry)
"Industries prescribed by Presidential Decree" in subparagraph 8 of Article 3 of the Act means industries falling under any of the following subparagraphs:
1. Industries that produce, process, manufacture, or cook products by processing agricultural or fishery products;
2. Industries that package, store, transport, or sell products produced by the industries under subparagraph 1.

Article 6-2 (Scope of Agribusiness)
"Industries prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as agricultural product processing and distribution, services related to agriculture and rural communities, and industries supplying agricultural inputs" in subparagraph 8-3 of Article 3 of the Act means industries falling under any of the following subparagraphs:
1. Agricultural product processing and distribution;
2. Services related to agriculture and rural communities;
3. Industries supplying agricultural inputs.

Article 7 (Criteria for Support for Organizations Related to Agriculture and the Food Industry)
The criteria for providing support for the operation of organizations related to agriculture and the food industry or for the installation and operation of facilities under Article 11 (3) of the Act shall be as follows: <Amended on March 23, 2013; December 22, 2015; April 28, 2016>
1. An organization eligible for support shall fall under any of the following items:
(a) A non-profit corporation established with permission from the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries under Article 32 of the Civil Act;
(b) A public-interest corporation established with permission from the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries under Article 4 of the Act on the Establishment and Operation of Public Interest Corporations;
(c) A producers' organization.
2. The scope of expenses eligible for support shall be as follows:
(a) Project expenses necessary for the members of a relevant organization to jointly fulfill the respective responsibilities prescribed in Article 4 of the Act;
(b) Expenses for constructing facilities or purchasing equipment and materials necessary for education and training, management guidance, and counseling for members of the organization, farmers, fishers, etc.
3. Matters other than the support criteria under subparagraphs 1 and 2 shall be governed by the Subsidy Management Act.

Article 8 (Formulation of Development Plans for Agriculture, Rural Communities, and the Food Industry)
(1) When formulating development plans for agriculture, rural communities, and the food industry under Article 14 of the Act, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, Metropolitan City Mayors, Do Governors, and Special Self-Governing Province Governors (hereinafter referred to as "Mayors/Do Governors"), and heads of Sis, Guns, and Gus (with heads of Gus referring to heads of autonomous Gus in Metropolitan Cities; hereinafter referred to as "heads of Sis/Guns/Gus") shall undergo deliberation by the Agriculture, Rural Community and Food Industry Policy Deliberation Councils at the respective levels under Article 15 of the Act. The same shall apply when amending such plans. <Amended on March 23, 2013; December 22, 2015>
(2) When the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs has formulated a development plan for agriculture, rural communities, and the food industry under Article 14 (1) of the Act, the Minister shall notify the Mayors/Do Governors thereof; and when a Mayor/Do Governor has formulated a development plan for agriculture, rural communities, and the food industry of a Metropolitan City, Do, or Special Self-Governing Province (hereinafter referred to as a "City/Do") under Article 14 (5) of the Act, the Mayor/Do Governor shall notify the heads of Sis/Guns/Gus thereof. <Amended on March 23, 2013; December 22, 2015>

Article 9 (Composition of the Central Agriculture, Rural Community and Food Industry Policy Deliberation Council)
(1) The Central Agriculture, Rural Community and Food Industry Policy Deliberation Council under Article 15 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Central Agricultural Policy Deliberation Council") shall be composed of not more than 20 members, including one chairperson and one vice chairperson. <Amended on March 23, 2013; December 22, 2015>
(2) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs shall serve as the chairperson of the Central Agricultural Policy Deliberation Council, and the vice chairperson shall be elected by and from among its members. <Amended on March 23, 2013; December 22, 2015>
(3) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs shall commission the following persons as members of the Central Agricultural Policy Deliberation Council: <Amended on March 23, 2013; December 22, 2015>
1. Not more than 12 heads of producers' organizations, farmers' organizations, consumers' organizations, and organizations related to the food industry;
2. Not more than seven persons who serve or have served as professors, research fellows, or in higher positions at universities, research institutes, or international organizations related to agriculture, rural communities, and the food industry, or who have worked for at least 10 years, currently or in the past, at organizations, administrative agencies, business entities, etc. related to agriculture, rural communities, and the food industry.
(4) The term of office of a member under paragraph (3) shall be two years.
(5) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs may dismiss a member under paragraph (3) from the commission if the member falls under any of the following subparagraphs: <Newly Inserted on December 31, 2015>
1. Where the member becomes unable to perform his or her duties due to a mental or physical disability;
2. Where the member has committed misconduct related to his or her duties;
3. Where the member is deemed unsuitable as a member due to neglect of duties, impairment of dignity, or any other reason;
4. Where the member personally expresses that he or she has difficulty performing the duties.
(6) Subcommittees for specialized fields may be established within the Central Agricultural Policy Deliberation Council to efficiently carry out the Council's affairs. <Newly Inserted on September 5, 2016>

Article 10 (Duties)
(1) The chairperson shall represent the Central Agricultural Policy Deliberation Council (hereinafter referred to as the "Central Policy Deliberation Council") and exercise overall control over its affairs. <Amended on March 23, 2013; December 22, 2015>
(2) The vice chairperson shall assist the chairperson and act on behalf of the chairperson where the chairperson is unable to perform his or her duties due to an unavoidable reason.

Article 11 (Meetings)
(1) The chairperson shall convene and preside over meetings of the Central Policy Deliberation Council. <Amended on March 23, 2013; December 22, 2015>
(2) A meeting of the Central Policy Deliberation Council shall commence with a majority of all incumbent members present, and resolutions shall be adopted with the consent of a majority of the members present. <Amended on March 23, 2013>

Article 12 (Allowances)
Allowances and travel expenses may be paid to members, etc. of the Central Policy Deliberation Council within budgetary limits: Provided, That this shall not apply where a member, etc. who is a public official attends the Central Policy Deliberation Council in direct connection with the affairs under his or her jurisdiction. <Amended on March 23, 2013>

Article 13 (Detailed Rules for Operation)
Matters necessary for the operation of the Central Policy Deliberation Council and its subcommittees, other than those prescribed in this Decree, shall be determined by the chairperson following a resolution of the Central Policy Deliberation Council. <Amended on March 23, 2013; September 5, 2016>

Article 14 (Composition of City/Do Agriculture, Rural Community and Food Industry Policy Deliberation Councils)
(1) Each City/Do Agriculture, Rural Community and Food Industry Policy Deliberation Council under Article 15 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "City/Do Policy Deliberation Council") shall be composed of not more than 25 members, including one chairperson and two vice chairpersons. <Amended on March 23, 2013; December 22, 2015>
(2) The Mayor/Do Governor shall serve as the chairperson, one of the vice chairpersons shall be the Deputy Mayor or Deputy Governor, and the other vice chairperson shall be elected by and from among the members under paragraph (3) 2 and 3.
(3) The Mayor/Do Governor shall commission the following persons as members: <Amended on December 22, 2015>
1. Not more than three heads of relevant administrative agencies;
2. Not more than 11 heads of producers' organizations, farmers' organizations, consumers' organizations, and organizations related to the food industry;
3. Not more than nine persons who serve or have served as associate professors, associate research fellows, or in higher positions at universities, research institutes, or international organizations related to agriculture, rural communities, and the food industry, or who have worked for at least seven years, currently or in the past, at organizations, administrative agencies, business entities, etc. related to agriculture, rural communities, and the food industry.
(4) The term of office of a member under paragraph (3) shall be two years.
(5) Subcommittees for individual commodities or functions may be established within a City/Do Policy Deliberation Council to efficiently carry out its affairs. <Amended on March 23, 2013>
(6) Matters necessary for the composition and operation of subcommittees under paragraph (5) shall be determined by the chairperson following a resolution of the City/Do Policy Deliberation Council. <Amended on March 23, 2013>

Article 14-2 (Dismissal of Members)
A Mayor/Do Governor may dismiss a member under Article 14 (3) from the commission if the member falls under any of the following subparagraphs:
1. Where the member becomes unable to perform his or her duties due to a mental or physical disability;
2. Where the member has committed misconduct related to his or her duties;
3. Where the member is deemed unsuitable as a member due to neglect of duties, impairment of dignity, or any other reason;
4. Where the member personally expresses that he or she has difficulty performing the duties.

Article 15 (Composition of Si/Gun/Gu Agriculture, Rural Community and Food Industry Policy Deliberation Councils)
(1) Each Si/Gun/Gu Agriculture, Rural Community and Food Industry Policy Deliberation Council under Article 15 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "Si/Gun/Gu Policy Deliberation Council") shall be composed of not more than 35 members, including one chairperson and two vice chairpersons. <Amended on March 23, 2013; December 22, 2015>
(2) The head of the Si/Gun/Gu shall serve as the chairperson, one of the vice chairpersons shall be the deputy head of the Si, Gun, or Gu (with the deputy head of a Gu referring to the deputy head of an autonomous Gu in a Metropolitan City), and the other vice chairperson shall be elected by and from among the members under paragraph (3) 2 through 4.
(3) The head of the Si/Gun/Gu shall commission the following persons as members: <Amended on December 22, 2015>
1. Not more than three heads of relevant administrative agencies;
2. Not more than 11 heads of producers' organizations, farmers' organizations, consumers' organizations, and organizations related to the food industry;
3. Not more than six persons who serve or have served as assistant professors, researchers, or in higher positions at universities, research institutes, or international organizations related to agriculture, rural communities, and the food industry, or who have worked for at least five years, currently or in the past, at organizations, administrative agencies, business entities, etc. related to agriculture, rural communities, and the food industry;
4. Not more than 13 representatives of farmers or food industry workers capable of leading local agriculture and the food industry.
(4) The term of office of a member under paragraph (3) shall be two years.
(5) Subcommittees for individual commodities or functions may be established within a Si/Gun/Gu Policy Deliberation Council to efficiently carry out its affairs. <Amended on March 23, 2013>
(6) Matters necessary for the composition and operation of subcommittees under paragraph (5) shall be determined by the chairperson following a resolution of the Si/Gun/Gu Policy Deliberation Council. <Amended on March 23, 2013>

Article 15-2 (Dismissal of Members)
The head of a Si/Gun/Gu may dismiss a member under Article 15 (3) from the commission if the member falls under any of the following subparagraphs:
1. Where the member becomes unable to perform his or her duties due to a mental or physical disability;
2. Where the member has committed misconduct related to his or her duties;
3. Where the member is deemed unsuitable as a member due to neglect of duties, impairment of dignity, or any other reason;
4. Where the member personally expresses that he or she has difficulty performing the duties.

Article 16 (Application Mutatis Mutandis)
Articles 10 through 13 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the operation, etc. of the City/Do Policy Deliberation Councils and Si/Gun/Gu Policy Deliberation Councils under Articles 14 and 15. <Amended on March 23, 2013>

Article 16-2 (Recipients of Agricultural and Food Vouchers, Etc.)
(1) The term "a person who meets the requirements prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 23-3(1) of the Act means a person who meets all of the following requirements:
1. The person shall fall under either of the following items:
(a) A recipient of livelihood benefits under Article 7(1)1 of the National Basic Living Security Act who is a member, including the head of household, of a household to which any of the following persons belongs (such household members include foreigners who are recipients under Article 5-2 of the National Basic Living Security Act and are recorded in a resident registration record for the household pursuant to Article 6-2(1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Resident Registration Act):
    1) A person aged 34 or younger;
    2) A pregnant or postpartum woman under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Mother and Child Health Act;
(b) Any other person whom the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs recognizes as needing support for access to food due to economic or social constraints or other circumstances and specifies by public notice;
2. The person shall not be receiving benefits at a welfare facility under Article 32 of the National Basic Living Security Act.
(2) The term "data prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as data concerning certification of family relationships and national and local taxes" in the former part of Article 23-3(2) of the Act means the following data:
1. Computerized information data under Article 11(4) of the Act on Registration of Family Relations (limited to information on registered matters recorded in a family relations certificate);
2. Data on whether a person is a recipient under subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the National Basic Living Security Act or belongs to the second-lowest income bracket under subparagraph 10 of that Article;
3. A medical certificate issued by a medical institution certifying that a person is a pregnant or postpartum woman under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Mother and Child Health Act;
4. Computerized resident registration information data under Article 30(1) of the Resident Registration Act;
5. Data concerning alien registration under Article 31 of the Immigration Act;
6. Other data that the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs recognizes as necessary to verify eligibility as a recipient of agricultural and food vouchers and the appropriateness of the amount received, and specifies by public notice.

Article 16-3 (Procedures for Providing Agricultural and Food Vouchers)
(1) A person who intends to receive agricultural and food vouchers pursuant to Article 23-3(1) of the Act shall submit an application for agricultural and food vouchers to the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the head of a Si (including the mayor of an administrative city under Article 11(2) of the Special Act on the Establishment of Jeju Special Self-Governing Province and the Development of Free International City), or the head of a Gun or autonomous Gu (hereinafter referred to as "mayor, etc."), as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs.
(2) Upon receiving an application for agricultural and food vouchers pursuant to paragraph (1), a mayor, etc. shall verify the data under Article 16-2(2), determine whether to provide agricultural and food vouchers, and notify the applicant in writing or by electronic means within 14 days from the date of application.
(3) Where a mayor, etc. has given notice of a decision to provide vouchers pursuant to paragraph (2), the mayor, etc. shall provide agricultural and food vouchers on a household basis.
(4) Matters necessary for applications for and the provision of agricultural and food vouchers, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (3), shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs.

Article 16-4 (Criteria and Procedures for Designating Dedicated Agencies, Etc.)
(1) An agency exclusively responsible for affairs relating to agricultural and food vouchers under Article 23-4(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "dedicated agency") shall meet all of the following criteria:
1. It shall be an agency or corporation that professionally performs affairs relating to agriculture and food and falls under either of the following items:
(a) A public institution under Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions;
(b) A nonprofit corporation established pursuant to the Civil Act or another Act;
2. It shall secure a dedicated organizational unit and dedicated personnel necessary to perform the affairs specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph (3);
3. It shall possess the technical capability to establish and operate an information system for performing affairs relating to the issuance and operation of agricultural and food vouchers.
(2) A person who intends to be designated as a dedicated agency shall submit to the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs an application for designation as a dedicated agency prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, accompanied by the documents prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs.
(3) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs may entrust a dedicated agency with the performance of the following affairs:
1. Establishment and operation of the information system under Article 23-4(1) of the Act;
2. Issuance and operation of agricultural and food vouchers;
3. Cooperation with local governments, agricultural and food suppliers, financial institutions, and others in relation to agricultural and food vouchers;
4. Surveys, research, education, and publicity relating to agricultural and food vouchers;
5. Compilation and management of statistics relating to agricultural and food vouchers.
(4) Where the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs designates a dedicated agency, the Minister shall publicly announce the name of the designated agency, the affairs entrusted to it, and other relevant matters on the website of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs.
(5) Details necessary for the designation and operation of dedicated agencies, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (4), shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs.

Article 16-5 (Revocation of Designation of Dedicated Agencies, Etc.)
(1) The detailed criteria for revoking the designation of a dedicated agency or suspending its operations under Article 23-4(2) of the Act shall be as specified in Attached Table 1.
(2) Where the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs revokes the designation of a dedicated agency, the Minister shall publicly announce that fact on the website of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs.
(3) Details necessary for revoking the designation of dedicated agencies and related matters, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) and (2), shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs.

Article 16-6 (Criteria for Imposing Penalty Surcharges) The criteria for imposing penalty surcharges according to the types and severity of violations and other relevant factors under Article 23-5(1) of the Act shall be as specified in Attached Table 2.

Article 16-7 (Imposition and Payment of Penalty Surcharges)
(1) When imposing a penalty surcharge pursuant to Article 23-5(1) of the Act, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs shall give written notice clearly stating the type of violation and the amount of the penalty surcharge.
(2) A person who receives notice pursuant to paragraph (1) shall pay the penalty surcharge to a collecting institution designated by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs within 20 days from the date of receipt of the notice.
(3) A collecting institution that receives a penalty surcharge pursuant to paragraph (2) shall issue a receipt to the person who paid the penalty surcharge.
(4) Upon receiving a penalty surcharge pursuant to paragraph (2), the collecting institution shall notify the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs of that fact without delay.
(5) Matters necessary for the procedures for collecting penalty surcharges, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (4), shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs.

Article 17 (Formulation of Comprehensive Plans for Promoting Technology, Research, Etc. Relating to Agriculture, the Food Industry, and Other Agricultural Industries)
(1) Pursuant to Article 35 of the Act, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs shall establish medium- and long-term objectives for technology development and research relating to agriculture, the food industry, and other agricultural industries, the establishment of a system for evaluating and managing such development and research, and other relevant matters, and shall formulate a comprehensive plan necessary to invigorate private and public research and development and promote the practical application of the results of technology development and research relating to agriculture, the food industry, and other agricultural industries (hereinafter referred to as a "comprehensive plan"). <Amended on March 23, 2013; December 22, 2015; June 23, 2026>
(2) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs may request the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and the heads of local governments to provide data on the current state of technology and technological forecasts in their respective areas of jurisdiction, and other data necessary to formulate a comprehensive plan pursuant to paragraph (1). <Amended on March 23, 2013; December 22, 2015>
(3) Upon formulating a comprehensive plan, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs shall notify the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and the heads of local governments thereof. <Amended on March 23, 2013; December 22, 2015>
(4) The Administrator of the Rural Development Administration and the Minister of the Korea Forest Service shall each formulate and implement an implementation plan for agricultural technology development and research under their respective jurisdictions, the practical application thereof, and the promotion of its dissemination, in accordance with the comprehensive plan.
(5) The Administrator of the Rural Development Administration and the Minister of the Korea Forest Service shall report the implementation plans formulated pursuant to paragraph (4) to the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs and notify Mayors/Do Governors thereof. <Amended on March 23, 2013>

Article 17-2 (Content of Surveys on the Current State of Information and Communications Technology Convergence, Etc.)
(1) A survey on the current state of information and communications technology convergence under Article 36-2(2)1 of the Act shall include the following matters:
1. Domestic and international market trends relating to information and communications technology convergence;
2. The current state of workforce supply and demand and employment relating to information and communications technology convergence;
3. The level of technology and the current state of its dissemination relating to information and communications technology convergence;
4. Other matters necessary for formulating and implementing policies relating to information and communications technology convergence.
(2) Where the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs intends to conduct a survey on the current state, the Minister shall prepare a survey plan that includes the purpose, subjects, methods, period, and other relevant matters of the survey, and shall notify the persons subject to the survey in advance.
(3) A survey on the current state shall be conducted by such methods as field surveys, written surveys, statistical surveys, or literature surveys, and, where necessary to conduct the survey efficiently, electronic means such as information and communications networks or electronic mail may be used.
(4) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs may commission a research institute, corporation, or organization with expertise in information and communications technology convergence to conduct a survey on the current state.

Article 18 (Details of Support for Farmers' Changes of Occupation and Reemployment) The term "matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 39(5)4 of the Act means the following matters: <Amended on March 23, 2013; December 22, 2015; March 25, 2025>
1. Matters concerning the payment of vocational training allowances, including vocational training preparation grants, vocational training expenses, and family living expenses;
2. Matters concerning the payment of employment preparation grants;
3. Other matters prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or by ordinance of the relevant local government for farmers' changes of occupation and reemployment.

Article 19 (Entrustment of Affairs Concerning the Promotion of the Public-Interest Functions of Agriculture and Rural Communities) The term "a person prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 46(3) of the Act means a person who falls under any of the following subparagraphs: <Amended on January 25, 2012; March 23, 2013; December 22, 2015; June 23, 2026>
1. The Korea Agency of Education, Promotion and Information Service in Food, Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries under Article 11-2 of the Act;
2. A research institute relating to agriculture, rural communities, and the food industry, or an affiliated institution of such a research institute, established pursuant to Article 8(1) of the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Research Institutes, Etc.;
3. A nonprofit corporation established with permission from the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs pursuant to Article 32 of the Civil Act;
4. A public-interest corporation established with permission from the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs pursuant to Article 4 of the Act on the Establishment and Operation of Public Interest Corporations;
5. The following corporations:
(a) The Korea Agro-Fisheries and Food Trade Corporation under the Korea Agro-Fisheries and Food Trade Corporation Act;
(b) The Korea Rural Community Corporation under the Korea Rural Community Corporation and Farmland Management Fund Act;
(c) Producers' organizations.

Article 19-2 (Delegation and Entrustment of Authority and Affairs Concerning Assessments of the Impacts of Climate Change on Agriculture and Rural Communities and Their Vulnerability, Etc.)
(1) Pursuant to Article 47-2(5) of the Act, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs shall delegate to the Administrator of the Rural Development Administration the authority concerning the following matters in the fields of agriculture and rural communities (excluding forestry and the fields of water for agricultural and fishing villages and agricultural production infrastructure under subparagraphs 3 and 6 of Article 2 of the Agricultural and Fishing Villages Improvement Act):
1. Surveys and assessments of the impacts of climate change on agriculture and rural communities and their vulnerability to climate change under Article 47-2(1) of the Act;
2. Surveys on the current state under Article 47-2(2) of the Act;
3. Requests for the provision of data or cooperation in surveys on the current state under Article 47-2(3) of the Act.
(2) Pursuant to Article 47-2(5) of the Act, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs shall delegate to the Minister of the Korea Forest Service the authority concerning the matters specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) in the fields of agriculture and rural communities (limited to forestry).
(3) Pursuant to Article 47-2(5) of the Act, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs shall entrust the Korea Rural Community Corporation under the Korea Rural Community Corporation and Farmland Management Fund Act with the affairs specified in paragraph (1)1 and 2 in the fields of agriculture and rural communities (limited to the fields of water for agricultural and fishing villages and agricultural production infrastructure under subparagraphs 3 and 6 of Article 2 of the Agricultural and Fishing Villages Improvement Act).

Article 20 (Matters Concerning Support for Providers of Information Relating to Agriculture and Rural Areas)
(1) Pursuant to Article 52(2) of the Act, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, a Mayor/Do Governor, and the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may subsidize the following expenses of providers of information relating to agriculture and rural areas within budgetary limits: <Amended on March 23, 2013; December 22, 2015; March 25, 2025>
1. Expenses incurred in providing farmers with information relating to agricultural management, technology, distribution, and other matters;
2. Expenses relating to informatization education and training for farmers and rural residents;
3. Expenses for informatization facilities in rural areas, such as computers and high-speed Internet;
4. Expenses for developing and disseminating software and other resources relating to agriculture and rural areas;
5. Other expenses necessary to promote informatization in agriculture and rural areas and prescribed by ordinance of the relevant local government (limited to cases where a local government provides support).
(2) Detailed procedures and criteria for selecting those eligible for support under paragraph (1), the purposes for which the support funds may be used, project implementing agencies, and other necessary matters shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or prescribed by ordinance of the relevant local government. <Amended on March 23, 2013; December 22, 2015; March 25, 2025>

Article 20-2 (Requests for the Provision of Information, Etc.)
(1) The term "information prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 52-2(2)6 of the Act means the information specified in Attached Table 3. <Amended on January 20, 2026>
(2) Where the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs deems it necessary to ensure that information requested pursuant to Article 52-2(2) of the Act is provided efficiently, the Minister may, in consultation with the Minister of National Court Administration, the heads of relevant central administrative agencies, the heads of local governments, and the heads of other relevant institutions and organizations (hereinafter referred to as "heads of relevant institutions and organizations"), promote the linkage of the integrated agriculture and rural community information system under Article 52-2(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "integrated agriculture and rural community information system") with information systems relating to the information specified in the subparagraphs of Article 52-2(2) of the Act.
(3) Where the head of a relevant institution or organization requests the use of information in the integrated agriculture and rural community information system pursuant to Article 52-2(3) of the Act, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs may provide information within the scope of the purposes of the affairs specified in paragraph (1) of that Article by such means as allowing the head of the relevant institution or organization to use the integrated agriculture and rural community information system or linking that system with the information system of the relevant institution or organization.

Article 21 (Support for Quasi-Rural Areas)
(1) Pursuant to Article 61 of the Act, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs may determine detailed project descriptions and conditions for support and provide necessary support for any of the following projects implemented in agricultural promotion areas under the Farmland Act and development restriction zones under the Act on Special Measures for Designation and Management of Development Restriction Zones that are located outside rural communities: <Amended on March 23, 2013; December 22, 2015>
1. Projects concerning innovation in agricultural management and the development of human resources;
2. Projects concerning agricultural mechanization and the modernization of facilities;
3. Projects concerning support for the distribution, processing, and export of agricultural products;
4. Projects concerning the diversification of income sources;
5. Projects concerning support for forestry and mountain villages;
6. Projects concerning the reinforcement, renovation, or repair of farm roads and agricultural irrigation facilities;
